message: "If you use this paper, its code or its data, please cite it as below."
title: "When does sequence order matter? An information-theoretic taxonomy of biological representation tasks and the limits of local encoding"
abstract: >-
  For any sequence of length L over an alphabet of size q and any local order k, the
  specification cost L log2 q splits exactly into a composition constraint, the resolved
  local-syntax information and the unresolved global-order entropy B_k(S), the fibre entropy
  of the k-spectrum. Evaluated across the 20,431 reviewed human proteins for 1 <= k <= 50, the
  decomposition separates globular enzymes and receptors (median spectral resolution 5, 95th
  percentile 7) from C2H2 zinc-finger regulators, structural tandem repeats and hominoid
  segmental-duplication arrays, which form the tail of unresolved order at k > 10. Combined
  with the seven companion studies, this yields a decision rule for choosing sequence
  encoders in computational biology.
